117
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:55.650
Do it.

118
00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:04.370
Michael is unable to pick a new chair for his office after he becomes infatuated with a woman modeling office chairs in a catalog.

119
00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.970
He decides he's ready to rejoin the dating pool.

120
00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:11.010
He's inspired by his love of the chair model.

121
00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:19.770
Meanwhile, the Dunder Mifflin parking spaces are being used by a construction crew doing work for W.B.

122
00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:20.430
Jones.

123
00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.350
And the staff of Dunder Mifflin has to park in a satellite lot.

124
00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:32.910
Kevin and Andy join forces to confront the other bosses of the Scranton Business Park to reclaim their places in the main lot.

125
00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:32.990
Right.

126
00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:34.690
Simple storylines.

127
00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:36.030
Absolute perfection.

128
00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.190
Also, absolutely true.

129
00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:38.830
Yeah.

130
00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:43.670
You know, I had to park in a satellite lot when I had my admin job.

131
00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:51.250
If you were an executive, you could park in the parking lot of the building, this high rise in downtown Los Angeles.

132
00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:51.910
Yeah.

133
00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.010
I did not get a parking space.

134
00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.770
I had to park in a satellite lot that was about a half a mile away.

135
00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:02.030
And it wasn't a big deal, except for on days that it rained.

136
00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:03.150
Well, here's the thing.

137
00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.910
You know where you rank in a company by where you park.

138
00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:06.770
Oh, yeah.

139
00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.910
Well, Michael has a spot right up front.

140
00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:09.930
He's not concerned.

141
00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:14.870
Well, later he's going to try to imagine what it would be like if he didn't have that spot.

142
00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:15.730
Mm-hmm.

143
00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:20.930
But it takes two employees angrily confronting him for him to get there.

144
00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:23.430
What are our fast facts, Jenna?

145
00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:30.470
Fast fact number one, the original title of this episode was Michael Dating.

146
00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.470
Hmm.

147
00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:38.910
But they realized that they would have to release the name of the episode before Dinner Party had aired.

148
00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:40.950
Oh, and they didn't want that.

149
00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:44.350
They didn't want to ruin the surprise about Jan and Michael's breakup, right?

150
00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:45.150
Yes.

151
00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:48.290
So they changed the name of the episode to Parking.

152
00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:54.490
But then they felt, well, while it's a big part of the episode, it's not the main storyline of the episode.

153
00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:58.910
So they eventually settled on Chair Model, which I personally love.

154
00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:03.130
I mean, when you see that, aren't you intrigued?

155
00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:04.390
I'm so intrigued.

156
00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.450
What is this episode going to be about?

157
00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:07.310
Chair Model.

158
00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.710
I'm more intrigued than Parking, I'll say that.

159
00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:11.290
They picked the right one.

160
00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:12.070
All right.

161
00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:18.230
Fast fact number two, I think we need to do a little breakdown of the Scranton Business Park Five Families.

162
00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:20.430
I mean, it's like Goodfellas.

163
00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:21.470
It is.

164
00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:22.930
All right.

165
00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:25.170
Here are the bosses of the five families.

166
00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:26.930
Michael Scott, of course.

167
00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.250
Bob Vance, Vance Refrigeration.

168
00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:31.190
Then there is W.B.

169
00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:32.830
Jones from W.B.

170
00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:33.770
Jones Heating and Air.

171
00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:35.930
He was played by Barry Sigismundi.

172
00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:41.270
Bill Kress of Kress, Tool & Die, who was played by Terrence Beeser.

173
00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:49.810
And finally, Paul Faust, of Disaster Kits Limited, who was played by Paul FauSt.

174
00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:59.470
This is an amazing story, and it all starts when an actual man named Paul Faust came to visit the set of The Office.

175
00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:00.530
Yes.

176
00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:02.870
So I have known Paul Faust for years.

177
00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:08.030
We used to be related by marriage, and I reached out to him, and he is just such a hoot.

178
00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:12.570
And he sent in all of these fantastic audio clips about his day on set.

179
00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:14.070
They are amazing.

180
00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:19.310
And in this first one, he explains exactly how he got the role on the show.

181
00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:21.370
Sam, you got to play this clip.

182
00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.910
Hey, office ladies and office fans.

183
00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:26.690
This is Paul Faust, a.k.a.

184
00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:27.570
Cool Guy Paul.

185
00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.530
I want to tell you how I got a part of the show.

186
00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:30.850
So I was out in L.A.

187
00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:36.530
on business and decided to visit the set because my cousins are part of the office.

188
00:08:36.530 --> 00:08:42.870
And when I was out there, I met the writers, and I kind of told them one of my companies and what I did, which was a company that sells disaster kits.

189
00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:45.190
And Greg had said, this is my cousin.

190
00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:46.470
He's a little nuts.

191
00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:47.210
Tell him what you do.

192
00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.610
I told him about the company.

193
00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.110
And when I walked out of the room, unbeknownst to me, B.J.

194
00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:56.390
Novak said, that guy's got to be a character on the show.

195
00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:03.730
So he wrote a part based on me, and the day before they shot the scene, they hadn't found an actor to play the part.

196
00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:07.810
So I got a call asking me if I wanted to audition to play the part.

197
00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:14.350
And long story short, I got the part, got to fly out and be part of the awesome show and play myself.

198
00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:17.250
So that's how I got to be on the show.

199
00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:19.590
It's really a story only can happen in Hollywood.

200
00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:21.570
I mean, that's bonkers.

201
00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:22.590
I know.

202
00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.230
He's literally playing himself.

203
00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:28.870
But what's bonkers to me about it was that they did hold auditions.

204
00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.110
They held auditions for this role.

205
00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.970
And the night before, they just had not found someone who could play Paul FauSt.

206
00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:37.890
So they're like, how about Paul Faust?

207
00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.250
Paul really sells disaster kits.

208
00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:41.330
That's real.

209
00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:42.870
That is his company.

210
00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:43.790
Oh, yeah.

211
00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.450
And Angela, he talked about that in this next clip.

212
00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:51.850
I think one of the most unique things about me being in the office was that I actually played myself.

213
00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.070
When I went out to L.A.

214
00:09:54.090 --> 00:10:01.810
and I was visiting and I went to the set, I talked about one of the businesses I run, which is a company called 1-800-PREPARE, 1-800-PREPARE.com,

215
00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:05.250
where we actually sell disaster kits and preparedness kits.

216
00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:09.430
So I talked about it, and that's what DJ had picked up on.

217
00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:14.150
And so he wrote a part called Cool Guy Paul, the owner of Disaster Kits Limited.

218
00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:17.170
And, yes, I still own that company.

219
00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:18.750
We still sell disaster kits.

220
00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:27.810
So I think I'm one of the few people who played himself on TV with his real name in a real business that he runs, which was kind of cool.

221
00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:33.330
So we asked Paul, how did he prepare, you know, to have these scenes?

222
00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:34.370
He's not an actor.

223
00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:40.410
He's not used to learning lines or where to sit, you know, how to navigate a set and all that kind of stuff.

224
00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:41.610
And here's what he had to say.

225
00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:47.710
So I got this part, let's say, on a Thursday, and I had, we were shooting Friday morning.

226
00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:51.750
So I had about a good 10 hours to learn my five or six lines.

227
00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:54.810
So I'm studying, studying, on the plane, I'm reading them.

228
00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:56.370
I didn't sleep, I studied them.

229
00:10:56.510 --> 00:11:00.970
So we go there, and this whole experience happens, and you're nervous, and there's a lot of people around.

230
00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:05.190
And I had 10 hours to learn a bunch of lines, so I was able to do it.

231
00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.990
And we knocked it out, and I got it done, and I was like, great, I finished.

232
00:11:09.570 --> 00:11:13.650
And then all of a sudden, somebody came in, and they threw these new scripts down.

233
00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:15.350
They said, okay, here's the alternate ending.

234
00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:18.450
Let's take a five-minute break, learn our lines, and come back at you.

235
00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:20.310
And I'm like, wait, wait, what a minute?

236
00:11:20.490 --> 00:11:21.250
Five minutes, what?

237
00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:23.350
I had 10 hours to learn my lines.

238
00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:25.590
Five minutes, I'm not learning new lines.

239
00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:31.210
So I raced out the hallway, had to study, learn all the lines, learn when I came in and whatever.

240
00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.670
I wound up doing it, knocking it out, getting it done right.

241
00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:43.850
And again, life experience with amazing people who were just so supportive, and I was glad that I was able to be a tiny part of this amazing family.

242
00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:49.630
Well, Angela, I found an interview with Paul, and he said that he landed in L.A.

243
00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:51.270
at 1.30 in the morning.

244
00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:56.230
The airline lost his luggage, so he had no luggage.

245
00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:02.150
He got to his hotel and went to sleep about 3.15 in the morning, and he had to be up at 5 a.m.

246
00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:02.770
for work.

247
00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:06.510
So after all of that, I'm kind of amazed.

248
00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:07.970
I would have been loopy.

249
00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:09.790
I wouldn't have been able to learn new lines.

250
00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:10.630
No.

251
00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:14.470
And I think of Paul, just like he's had no sleep.

252
00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:18.430
He's probably in the same underwear because they lost his bag.

253
00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:25.810
And he, you know, and now he's like, he thought he reached the finish line, and then they hand him this whole new task.

254
00:12:26.210 --> 00:12:30.330
Well, Paul sent a few more clips about his experience just being on set.

255
00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:31.210
They're so delightful.

256
00:12:31.430 --> 00:12:33.350
We're going to sprinkle those in throughout the episode.

257
00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:34.270
All right.

258
00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:36.090
Fast fact number three, Angela.

259
00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:41.190
I'm titling this, Am I the Inspiration for This Episode?

260
00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:43.270
I'm sorry, what?

261
00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:44.370
Were you a chair model?

262
00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:55.010
No, but in season one of the show, I wanted a new chair at reception, and I had to lobby for it.

263
00:12:55.210 --> 00:13:03.670
So I'm sure I'm not the inspiration for this episode, but in season one, they gave me a chair at reception without arMs.

264
00:13:03.910 --> 00:13:08.550
It was an armless chair, and I really, really wanted a chair with arMs.

265
00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:16.090
And so I attempted to try to sort of move the chairs or find a chair maybe that hadn't been claimed or used.

266
00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:17.410
It wasn't working.

267
00:13:17.650 --> 00:13:25.150
And big shout out to our set dresser, Steve Rothstein, because he is one of the guys who headed up the team that picked out all our furniture on the office,

268
00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.410
and he got me a chair with arMs.

269
00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:28.570
Oh, lady.

270
00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:30.170
And I was so excited.

271
00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:32.510
Well, I have a chair story for you.

272
00:13:32.510 --> 00:13:36.430
I've been holding on to this, but I'm going to go ahead and tell you now.

273
00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:39.330
The chair I'm sitting in right now, ready?

274
00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:40.530
I'm going to pan down.

275
00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:41.870
Do you see this chair?

276
00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:42.970
I do.

277
00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.170
It's a blue chair with mesh.

278
00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:46.870
Do you recognize it?

279
00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.890
I don't recognize it, but it has no arMs.

280
00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:52.190
Phil Shea gave me this chair.

281
00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:56.230
It was a leftover chair from Workbus.

282
00:13:56.870 --> 00:13:58.150
Oh, my gosh.

283
00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:01.290
You've been sitting in a Workbus chair this whole time?

284
00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:03.930
For a year in my closet.

285
00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:12.170
I have been sitting in my Workbus chair, and I was going to save it for Workbus, but we're having all this chair talk.

286
00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:13.550
I couldn't hold it in anymore.

287
00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:22.450
Well, I just have to say, this is why our show is so great, because getting a chair is a very relatable, big deal moment.

288
00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:30.790
When you are in an office job or any job, your chair, you sit in it all day, and they created this amazing, wonderful storyline out of it.

289
00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:35.350
Yes, and thank you, Phil Shea, because I had gotten a desk.

290
00:14:35.550 --> 00:14:40.970
You know, I had in the corner of my guest room a little desk where I would sit and do work, and I didn't have a proper desk chair.

291
00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:45.570
I was using one of the kitchen chairs, and I was sort of talking about it on set, and Phil Shea was like,

292
00:14:45.910 --> 00:14:47.790
Ange, I have some extra chairs from Workbus.

293
00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:48.690
Do you want one?

294
00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.030
I was like, yes!

295
00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:51.790
This is making me happy.

296
00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:52.650
I know.

297
00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:54.170
We all have a chair story.

298
00:14:54.330 --> 00:15:00.090
And I have been sitting in it for a year with you and haven't told you, because I was going to try to work it into Workbus.

299
00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:01.290
But there you go.

300
00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.830
Let's take a break, and when we get back, we'll get into it.

301
00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:05.590
All right.

302
00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:16.600
We are back.

303
00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:29.800
Outside of the Dunder Mifflin office, and they are explaining that due to some renovations in the building, all of their parking spots are being taken up,

304
00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:33.520
and they have to park in a satellite lot and walk to work.

305
00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.920
They're being really positive about it, though.

306
00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:40.240
Yeah, they saw a junkyard dog eating, you know, some of a chicken.

307
00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:46.140
Yeah, eating the bones of a rotisserie chicken, Angela, which was based on a real story.

308
00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:47.680
Yeah, it was.

309
00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:59.160
There was a junkyard next to our real office building, and there was a junkyard dog who they would throw, like, old rotisserie chicken bones to.

310
00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:02.000
We've talked about this junkyard and the dog.

311
00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:05.320
We got very invested in this dog's life that lived over there.

312
00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:06.080
We did.

313
00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.980
And it made it into an episode.

314
00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:12.020
So then we have Oscar and Andy, who are less positive.

315
00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:16.920
And then there's Kevin, who is just downright angry.

316
00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:21.040
Kevin is going to have a breakdown over this satellite parking lot.

317
00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:21.620
Yeah.

318
00:16:22.220 --> 00:16:26.520
Well, Andy says he's so bummed because he lost a penny out of his penny loafers.

319
00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:27.260
Yeah.

320
00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.500
Jenna, I don't understand penny loafers, so I looked them up.

321
00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:31.220
Oh.

322
00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:32.720
You could have asked me.

323
00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:36.100
I wore penny loafers all through high school as part of my school uniform.

324
00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:39.040
Okay, well, why did you put a penny in your penny loafer?

325
00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:42.460
I don't know, because there was a slot there for it.

326
00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:43.900
Okay, well, I'll tell you.

327
00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:45.080
I'll tell you why.

328
00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:46.820
Oh, my God.

329
00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:49.520
Well, you have put me in my place.

330
00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.200
I clearly don't know about penny loafers.

331
00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:53.780
You told me I could have asked you.

332
00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:55.840
Well, you're not answering my question.

333
00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.460
Why a penny in your loafers?

334
00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:05.320
So, according to the internets, back in the 1930s, the payphone in the phone booth cost

335
00:17:05.320 --> 00:17:06.660
two cents.

336
00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:13.460
The new loafer design allowed just enough space for a penny in each shoe, equaling the cost

337
00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:14.900
of an emergency phone call.

338
00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:17.980
Thus, the penny and the loafer were united.

339
00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:21.020
But only men could make emergency phone calls.

340
00:17:23.200 --> 00:17:25.980
If you were a woman, so sorry.

341
00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.440
You don't even get a pocket to put your pennies in.

342
00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:30.600
Good luck, lady.

343
00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:32.500
You're just going to die on the street.

344
00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:35.560
But for the men, we have pockets.

345
00:17:35.560 --> 00:17:37.720
You can hide money in your shoes.

346
00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:41.080
Where else can we make life easy for you, gentlemen?

347
00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:45.700
That took a turn, but point taken.

348
00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:51.760
So, now the episode starts, and Jenna, it would have started with a deleted scene with

349
00:17:51.760 --> 00:17:54.720
Michael checking out of the Shroop FarMs.

350
00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:56.360
What?

351
00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:57.120
Yeah.

352
00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:59.360
Oh, because he's been staying with Dwight?

353
00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:00.120
Yes.

354
00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:04.940
So, there was an alternate beginning where Michael is leaving the farm, and he's like,

355
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:05.860
it's moving day.

356
00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:07.940
You know, I've been here about a month.

357
00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:12.700
I let Jan have the condo, and I'm moving out of Shroop FarMs.

358
00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:16.760
And as he's leaving, Dwight goes, hold up, and hands him a bill.

359
00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:21.900
And Michael's like, wait, you were charging me this whole time?

360
00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:23.600
And Dwight was like, yeah.

361
00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.680
And then they have this funny back and forth about it.

362
00:18:26.840 --> 00:18:27.820
Oh, my gosh.

363
00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:33.240
My mind is blown by the backstory that Michael lived with Dwight for a month.

364
00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:39.700
And it does sort of show why maybe now he's looking at the chair model like he is, because

365
00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:46.200
he's a month past his breakup, and I don't know, maybe he's ready for something new.

366
00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:47.280
Yeah.

367
00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:50.580
Oh, can we see this deleted scene somewhere?

368
00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.100
Yes, it's on the DVDs.

369
00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:54.040
Amazing.

370
00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:57.880
So the scene they went with is this one in his office.

371
00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:04.400
He's looking through the catalog and really just gazing at this lady in this chair.

372
00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:07.420
Well, we had some fan mail about it.

373
00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:12.260
Carly D., Jenna A., Mariah K., and Charlotte S.

374
00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:14.900
all wrote in to ask who played the chair

375
00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:16.500
model in the picture.

376
00:19:16.920 --> 00:19:19.600
The chair model was played by April Eden.

377
00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:23.040
She would go on to play Trish, a.k.a.

378
00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:26.600
Miss Pawnee, in two episodes of Parks and Recreation.

379
00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.060
Well, I have a little background catch.

380
00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:35.520
At a minute, nine seconds, there's a wonderful shot of the chair model, but the magazine is

381
00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.740
sitting on top of some message slips.

382
00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:38.600
Oh.

383
00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:41.900
And I zoomed in on the message slip.

384
00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:46.760
The message that was written down said, Andrea, oof or ooter, guys?

385
00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:47.360
I don't know.

386
00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:48.220
I couldn't make it out.

387
00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:53.260
So, from Dunder, Louisville, called and asked Michael to please call as soon as he returns.

388
00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:58.580
And the number was 307-555-4438.

389
00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:00.600
So I called it.

390
00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:02.820
Well, that's a fake number.

391
00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:07.060
Well, yeah, it rang fast busy, but I wasn't sure, so I called it.

392
00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:13.120
No, you know that thing where in movies they always start phone numbers with 555 because

393
00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.380
nobody has that as a prefix?

394
00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:20.800
Jenna, I know that, but we've had a phone number and it ended up being a sex hotline.

395
00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.340
So I decided to do my due diligence.

396
00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:30.780
It is a phony baloney number, but the area code 307 is a Wyoming area code, but there is

397
00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.040
no Louisville in Wyoming.

398
00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:33.460
There you have it.

399
00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:34.160
Boom!

400
00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:40.060
Everything you needed to know about this message slip next to the catalog.

401
00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:42.460
Angela, I admire you.

402
00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:44.280
I do.

403
00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:45.400
I do.

404
00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:46.320
I really love that detail.

405
00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:55.960
Pam just needs Michael to pick a chair so that she can get his chair, but he is now obsessed

406
00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:59.500
with the chair model and Pam is very sad.

407
00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:04.380
But then Michael has a talking head where he says he really likes being single because

408
00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:07.220
he can start off each day with a sense of possibility.

409
00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:14.100
And this was my favorite part when this talking head takes a turn, he says, and each day becomes

410
00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:22.360
more desperate and desperate situations yield the quickest results, which is kind of true.

411
00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:23.600
It is true.

412
00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:30.080
There was an alt talking head here that was pretty amazing.

413
00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:37.780
Jenna, you know the nursery rhyme where it's like, Pam and Jim sitting in a tree, K-I-S-S-I-N-G.

414
00:21:37.940 --> 00:21:38.460
You know that one?

415
00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:38.980
Yes.

416
00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:44.700
So he's sort of used this nursery rhyme as a template for how to find your perfect match.

417
00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.980
And Sam, can you play it?

418
00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:51.680
Love, marriage, baby carriage.

419
00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:57.560
Those have been my goals ever since I heard that song.

420
00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:00.520
Jenna and I had love.

421
00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:02.900
We did not have marriage.

422
00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:09.100
We did have a baby carriage, which I got her for bringing groceries home after she got

423
00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:09.740
a DUI.

424
00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:11.860
Oh my God.

425
00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:16.980
So messed up.

426
00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:23.140
And I have to say, Jenna, in this scene with you, when he says, remember after my dinner

427
00:22:23.140 --> 00:22:28.660
party, when I said that I was swearing off women, the way you said, I definitely remember

428
00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:31.400
your dinner party was so good.

429
00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:33.540
You just crushed it.

430
00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:36.540
I love doing that scene with Steve.

431
00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:41.040
I remembered when I was watching it, how much we were laughing.

432
00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:42.840
It was just so fun.

433
00:22:43.020 --> 00:22:44.300
That was a good memory.

434
00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:50.000
It's a very subtle, like, reminder to the audience what just came before this.

435
00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:50.740
Yeah.

436
00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:51.680
Okay.

437
00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:56.460
Well, now, Jenna, we have a Creed talking head that is phenomenal.

438
00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:58.340
It is phenomenal.

439
00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:05.360
He says, when Pam gets Michael's old chair, he gets Pam's old chair, which will bring him

440
00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:08.180
to two chairs, only one more to go.

441
00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:10.880
Why does he need so many chairs?

442
00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.180
You're not the only person who's wondering.

443
00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:14.800
We got a lot of mail about it.

444
00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.660
So I texted Creed.

445
00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:17.860
Oh, no.

446
00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:22.180
And he had a lot of answers for why he needed three chairs, and I'm going to share them all.

447
00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:22.860
Wait, wait.

448
00:23:22.940 --> 00:23:26.660
Is this sort of like Creed, the actor giving Creed the character backstory?

449
00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:27.460
Yes.

450
00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:28.340
Oh, I love it.

451
00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:29.260
All right.

452
00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:30.600
Option number one.

453
00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:32.780
He was growing weed in the ceiling.

454
00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:36.160
It was the same place where Angela threw her cat.

455
00:23:36.940 --> 00:23:42.720
Now, he had access through a crawl space outside, but he needed to get in there at night by

456
00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:43.460
his desk.

457
00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:48.640
So after finally getting his three chairs and climbing into the ceiling, they collapsed one

458
00:23:48.640 --> 00:23:50.580
night, and he was stranded in the ceiling.

459
00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.380
He wants you to know that's a whole thing that could have happened.

460
00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:56.960
Creed, what the hell are you talking about?

461
00:23:57.380 --> 00:23:59.920
You're going to stack three office chairs?

462
00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:02.400
I think all three of them had wheels.

463
00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:03.200
Yeah.

464
00:24:03.460 --> 00:24:05.160
Well, here's option number two.

465
00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:11.140
He said Creed stole many things from the office, and he needed the extra chairs for a set because

466
00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:13.680
he was selling it to a competitive paper company.

467
00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:15.100
That, I believe.

468
00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:15.640
Yes.

469
00:24:15.820 --> 00:24:18.340
I believe Creed had many side hustles.

470
00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:20.240
Well, here's his final answer.

471
00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:25.640
After perfecting his cartwheel, he wanted to use the three chairs as part of an obstacle

472
00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:27.480
course to really challenge himself.

473
00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.580
Well, that could have been very true.

474
00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.740
Oh, Creed, you're so fun.

475
00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:35.500
Thank you for humoring us time and time again.

476
00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:43.680
Well, Michael tells everyone in the bullpen, get out your catalogs, open them to page 85,

477
00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:46.500
and take a look at the woman in the catalog.

478
00:24:46.900 --> 00:24:50.120
Yeah, use that as a template for who you're going to set me up with.

479
00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:52.060
Jenna, this was an announcement.

480
00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:56.580
He came out of his office to address his employees.

481
00:24:56.580 --> 00:25:03.680
Could you imagine working with a boss that made a huge announcement about how you should

482
00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:04.840
go about setting him up?

483
00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:05.820
No.

484
00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:10.380
Later, he says if you don't provide him a name, by the end of the hour, you're going to be

485
00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:10.860
fired.

486
00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:12.320
We'll get there.

487
00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:13.660
But it's crazy.

488
00:25:13.660 --> 00:25:15.920
So now here's what I noticed.

489
00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:21.200
We all do get out these catalogs and we start opening up to page 85.

490
00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:23.620
And I remember these catalogs.

491
00:25:23.720 --> 00:25:24.920
