00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:52.880
So the camera guy behind me, he's like, you don't know what that means?

994
00:48:52.980 --> 00:48:54.900
I'm like, no, I've never done this before.

995
00:48:55.080 --> 00:48:58.960
He goes, OK, I'll tap you on the shoulder when you should say your line.

996
00:48:58.960 --> 00:49:01.540
So I was literally sitting there and something was going on.

997
00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:05.560
And I felt the camera guy who was shooting over my shoulder tap me on the shoulder when

998
00:49:05.560 --> 00:49:06.320
to say my line.

999
00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:10.300
And it was just sort of indicative of the whole experience because everybody was so friendly

1000
00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:12.540
and helpful and just wanted to get it done right.

1001
00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:17.520
Except, I think, Brian and Ed, who were just trying to make me laugh the whole time.

1002
00:49:18.200 --> 00:49:22.360
Can I say, Angela, that when we got these audio clips from Paul, the thing that was

1003
00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:31.840
so fun for me was his observations as a non-actor being on a set, because it really took me back

1004
00:49:31.840 --> 00:49:37.660
to all of those moments in my career as an actor where you just are learning on the fly.

1005
00:49:37.780 --> 00:49:40.560
I didn't learn any of this stuff in theater school.

1006
00:49:41.040 --> 00:49:43.980
And I don't even know if there is any other way to learn it.

1007
00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:45.600
You just kind of absorb it in the moment.

